    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Random Variables & Distributions: Models for How Data Arrive\"\ndescription: \"Connect Bernoulli, binomial, Poisson and continuous distributions to real data-generating processes and ML targets.\"\nauthor: \"MLStation\"\ndate: 2026-10-01\ncategories: [Statistics, Distributions, Probability]\ndraft: false\n---\n\n\nA distribution is not merely a curve. It is a **model for how values are generated**.\n\nChoosing a distribution begins with the mechanism:\n\n> Is the outcome binary? A count? A waiting time? A continuous measurement?\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n## What you will learn\n- Random variables, PMFs/PDFs and CDFs\n- Expectation and variance\n- Bernoulli, binomial and Poisson models\n- How parameters change distributions\n- How distribution choice appears in ML loss functions\n:::\n\n## Random variable\n\nA random variable maps uncertain outcomes to numbers.\n\nIts expectation is\n\n$$\nE[X]=\\sum_x xP(X=x)\n$$\n\nfor a discrete variable, or\n\n$$\nE[X]=\\int x f(x)\\,dx\n$$\n\nfor a continuous variable.\n\nVariance is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[(X-E[X])^2].\n$$\n\n## Interactive distribution laboratory\n\nChoose a probability $p$ and see the binomial count distribution change.\n\n```{ojs}\n//| echo: false\nviewof nbin = Inputs.range([5,100], {value:20, step:1, label:\"Trials n\"})\nviewof pbin = Inputs.range([0.02,.98], {value:.3, step:.01, label:\"Success probability p\"})\nlogFact = n => d3.sum(d3.range(1,n+1), d=>Math.log(d))\nchoose = (n,k)=>Math.exp(logFact(n)-logFact(k)-logFact(n-k))\nbinom = d3.range(nbin+1).map(k=>({k,p:choose(nbin,k)*pbin**k*(1-pbin)**(nbin-k)}))\nPlot.plot({\n height:320,x:{label:\"Number of successes\"},y:{label:\"Probability\"},\n marks:[Plot.barY(binom,{x:\"k\",y:\"p\",fill:\"#4F46E5\"}),Plot.ruleY([0])]\n})\n```\n\nFor $X\\sim\\text{Binomial}(n,p)$,\n\n$$\nE[X]=np,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X)=np(1-p).\n$$\n\n## Mechanism → distribution\n\n| Data-generating story | Common starting model |\n|---|---|\n| One yes/no outcome | Bernoulli |\n| Successes in fixed $n$ trials | Binomial |\n| Events in an interval | Poisson |\n| Symmetric continuous measurement | Normal |\n| Positive waiting time | Exponential/Gamma |\n\nThe model is an approximation. The story helps you choose a sensible starting point.\n\n## Simulation: Poisson counts\n\n::: {#a73eaa5a .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code code-fold=\"true\"}\nimport numpy as np\nimport plotly.express as px\nimport pandas as pd\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(4)\nrows=[]\nfor lam in [1,4,10]:\n    for x in rng.poisson(lam, 3000):\n        rows.append({\"lambda\":f\"λ={lam}\",\"count\":x})\ndf=pd.DataFrame(rows)\nfig=px.histogram(df,x=\"count\",facet_col=\"lambda\",histnorm=\"probability\",\n                 nbins=25)\nfig.update_layout(height=340,showlegend=False)\nfig.show()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div style=\"height:340px; width:100%;\">            <script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjax/3.2.2/es5/tex-svg.min.js\"></script><script>if (window.MathJax && window.MathJax.Hub && window.MathJax.Hub.Config) {window.MathJax.Hub.Config({SVG: {font: \"STIX-Web\"}});}</script>                <script>window.PlotlyConfig = {MathJaxConfig: 'local'};</script>\n        <script charset=\"utf-8\" src=\"https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-4.1.1.min.js\" integrity=\"sha256-O24V1F27f8pb0glCkelh3cVHLNiHAJ5gCaVtq2aNch8=\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>                <div id=\"58812839-43ac-4db9-92b3-adee48628a04\" class=\"plotly-graph-div\" style=\"height:100%; width:100%;\"></div>            <script>                window.PLOTLYENV=window.PLOTLYENV || {};                                if (document.getElementById(\"58812839-43ac-4db9-92b3-adee48628a04\")) {                    Plotly.newPlot(                        \"58812839-43ac-4db9-92b3-adee48628a04\",                        [{\"bingroup\":\"x\",\"histnorm\":\"probability\",\"hovertemplate\":\"lambda=\\u03bb=1\\u003cbr